@rustbot nominate +I-lang-nominated

Here is my write-up of the drop order questions. Based on the [tests from this PR](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/132833), I believe the way you can think of it is this:

Given an if-statement `if C0 && ... && Cn { B1 } else { B2 }` where each condition `Ci = $expr | let $pat = $expr` is either regular boolean expressions or a `let $pat = $expr` match:

* Any temporaries introduced as part of the `let` conditions in the `C` will be dropped on exit from the then-block `B1` or on entry to the else block `B2`.
* Any temporaries introduced in boolean expressions (which cannot possibly be referenced) will be dropped on entry to the then-block.

[This test](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/07b5eeebc948743eaadb32a83d23931fd8854fe8/tests/ui/drop/drop-order-comparisons.rs#L330-L345) demonstrates temporaries being dropped after exiting B1 and [this test](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/07b5eeebc948743eaadb32a83d23931fd8854fe8/tests/ui/drop/drop-order-comparisons.rs#L470-L485) demonstrates temporaries being dropped before entering B2.

This behavior is equivalent to what you get today with nested if-let chains, as demonstrated by [this test](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/07b5eeebc948743eaadb32a83d23931fd8854fe8/tests/ui/drop/drop-order-comparisons.rs#L270-L288) (then-block). (This equivalence doesn't hold, but also doesn't make sense, in the [else case](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/07b5eeebc948743eaadb32a83d23931fd8854fe8/tests/ui/drop/drop-order-comparisons.rs#L270-L288).)

This order is somewhat different from what you get ([test case](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/07b5eeebc948743eaadb32a83d23931fd8854fe8/tests/ui/drop/drop-order-comparisons.rs#L290-L311)) if you wrote a series of `let-else` statements with breaks like

```rust
'outer: {
    'else_blk: {
        if C1 else { break 'else_blk; };
        ...
        let Pat_i = Expr_i else { break 'else_blk; };  // Ci = let Pat_i = Expr_i
        ...
        if Cn else { break 'else_blk; };
        $then-block;
        break 'outer;
    }
    $else-block;
}
```

In that case, unbound values from the RHS are dropped as soon as you exit the statement `let Ok(_) = $expr else { break 'else_blk; }`. It's not clear that anybody would expect this equivalence to hold (or even think of it).

The TL;DR for me is -- the order is a bit crazy, but it aligns with nested if-let, and I think it's the best we can do right now that meets the various criteria. In general I think it is good to drop sooner vs later, and I would not (for example) want temporaries in boolean expressions to be dropped later just to align with temporaries from block patterns. We decided in Rust 2024 not to have "potentially referenced" temporaries in scenarios like `match some_lock.lock().unwrap().do_something()` drop sooner, and so that means that I would expect the temporaries for `if let` scenarios to cover the then-block, which means we are stuck with that inconsistency. This seems to be the best we can do.

